$ zenctl serve --help
Stand up a mock queryable: answer every query on a keyexpr with one static body, and log every ask (#121).

The log doubles as a "who is querying this key" probe. Deliberately no reply scripting — static and file bodies cover the dev-loop case; the shell covers dynamic replies by restarting serve. (nuze and zsak own the embedded-language lane, at the cost of a Nushell dependency and a linked libpython respectively.)

Usage: zenctl serve [OPTIONS] <KEYEXPR> <REPLY>

Arguments:
  <KEYEXPR>
          Key expression to serve (full wire form; wildcards welcome)

  <REPLY>
          Reply body: inline text, `@file`, or `-` for stdin (read once) — through the same encode ladder as `pub`

Options:
      --encoding <ENCODING>
          Wire encoding to declare on replies. Defaults to the registry's declared encoding when the keyexpr refines, else none

      --no-validate
          Do not refuse a body the served schema rejects

      --raw
          Reply the bytes verbatim: no schema lookup, no refusal

      --complete
          Declare the queryable complete — a claim this responder holds ALL the data the expression names. Say it only when you mean it

      --count <N>
          Exit after N queries (0 = until ctrl-c)
          
          [default: 0]

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl base list` discovers the bases in use.
          
