$ zenctl gen --help
Generate registry-driven test traffic (#162): every declared subject of a producer, schema-synthesized payloads, declared QoS, class-conscious rates — a mock producer for testing consumers.

The full plan prints BEFORE anything is published; every sample carries the RFC 09 §5.3 synthetic marker ({"synthetic":true,"tool":…,"origin":…}), so a doctor listen window or a capture can tell this traffic from real. Events stay inside their declared rate budget on write-once keys. A run wider than 10 subjects needs --wide; faults need --i-know.

Usage: zenctl gen [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --producer <PRODUCER>
          Only this producer's subjects

      --subject <SUBJECT>
          Only subjects whose declared path contains this

      --var <K=V>
          Value for a `{var}` in a declared path (repeatable, k=v). Unnamed vars get deterministic synthetic values, stated in the plan

      --origin <ORIGIN>
          Origin the generated keys claim (h-<12 hex>). Default: derived from this session's zid — printed either way, and stamped into the marker

      --rate <HZ>
          Override every entry's rate (Hz). Default: registry-driven — telemetry 1 Hz, state refreshes at ttl/2, events inside their declared budget

      --pattern <PATTERN>
          Send-timing shape (all deterministic under --seed)
          
          [default: steady]
          [possible values: steady, jitter, burst, ramp]

      --duration <SECS>
          How long to keep generating, seconds. The one `--duration` in the tool, and it bounds OUTPUT: a passive window is `--for` everywhere (#307)
          
          [default: 10]

      --seed <SEED>
          Synthesis/jitter seed — same seed, same run
          
          [default: 42]

      --fault <KIND>
          Inject fault(s) into otherwise-valid samples (#163) for consumer-robustness testing on a bus you own. Comma-separated kinds: truncate, wrong-type, extra-field, unregistered-key, wrong-qos, missing-encoding, unstamped. Each perturbs one dimension post-synthesis; the plan states the delta per key, and every faulted sample's marker carries fault=<kind> (RFC 09 §5.3). DOUBLE-GUARDED: requires --i-know AND an endpoint or --base TYPED on this command line — an exported ZENCTL_BASE or context default is the ambient bus the shell was pointed at, which is exactly what faults must never land on

      --schema-set <FILE>
          SchemaSet JSON document (RFC 08 §7) for payload shapes when the bus serves no describe (the registry carries type names, not shapes)

      --serve-describe
          Also answer introspect (and describe, with --schema-set) for the impersonated producers — a complete mock producer, not just a firehose

      --dry-run
          Print the plan and publish nothing

      --i-know
          Mean the faults: the acknowledging half of --fault's double guard

      --wide
          Acknowledge a run wider than 10 subjects — a fleet-wide impersonation

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl base list` discovers the bases in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.toml`), repeatable. Joined with the live bus as a union (RFC 08 §6.1): a producer's served slice wins, these files fill the gaps, and a disagreement is reported — never silently overwritten. With the bus unreachable they answer alone

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`)

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default, and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Reply-wait only: a passive observation window is `--for`

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5)
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl scout --help
Listen for raw scouting Hellos: zid, whatami, locators.

The layer *below* `base list`: no session is opened, so this answers "is anything out there at all" and "is multicast working on this segment". It is also the one zenctl verb where multicast is ON by default — scouting is the point, and a scout only listens for Hellos and joins nothing.

Usage: zenctl scout [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --what <WHAT>
          Filter by advertised kind. Repeatable; default: all three
          
          [possible values: router, peer, client]

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for Hellos (default 5; a context may override)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable — reaches gossip scouting where multicast is filtered

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context for endpoints/timeout defaults

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl completions --help
Generate shell completions (bash, zsh, fish, elvish, powershell).

e.g. `zenctl completions bash > ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/zenctl`

The default script is **dynamic** (issue #54): it calls back into `zenctl` so producer, subject, type and procedure names come from the cached registry of the active context. The cache is written by any command that loads slices; completion never touches the bus, so a `<TAB>` cannot hang on a fleet that is down. `--static` emits the old self-contained script instead.

Usage: zenctl completions [OPTIONS] <SHELL>

Arguments:
  <SHELL>
          [possible values: bash, elvish, fish, powershell, zsh]

Options:
      --static
          Emit the self-contained script: no callbacks, no cached names

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl doctor --help
Check the fleet against the contracts it claims (RFC 08 §6): drift, freshness, QoS, coverage.

RFC 08 §6: "A disagreement between introspection and the checked-in TOML is a finding, not an ambiguity." This prints the findings — `registry diff` shows the two registries side by side; doctor *judges* the deployment. The local truth comes from `--registry <dir>`; without it only the roster-vs-introspect check runs.

Usage: zenctl doctor [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --deep
          Additionally GET current state to check freshness against each subject's ttl (RFC 04 §1.2) and judge storage coverage — adds fleet query load

      --sample <N>
          With --deep: drain at most N state samples per family — bounds the sweep's cost, not just its output

      --for <SECS>
          Listen passively to the data planes for this many seconds after the GET fan-in and judge what rides (#161): undecodable/invalid payloads, declared-vs-observed QoS, unregistered traffic, over-rate events. The report states the window, its scopes, and what the bounded observer dropped (O5/O6)

      --fail-on <SEVERITY>
          Exit 1 when a finding at (or above) this severity exists. Default: always exit 0 — findings are output, not verdicts

          Possible values:
          - error:   Fail on error-severity findings only
          - warning: Fail on warnings or errors

      --transitions
          Re-run the checks on an interval and report CHECK-ID TRANSITIONS as ndjson (#227): the first run states the baseline (one line per stable check id, from null), every later run prints only genuine changes — and a run that fails flips every check to `unobservable`, never silently to "ok"

      --every <SECS>
          With --transitions: seconds between runs
          
          [default: 10]

      --count <N>
          With --transitions: stop after N runs (default: run until interrupted)

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl base list` discovers the bases in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.toml`), repeatable. Joined with the live bus as a union (RFC 08 §6.1): a producer's served slice wins, these files fill the gaps, and a disagreement is reported — never silently overwritten. With the bus unreachable they answer alone

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`)

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default, and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Reply-wait only: a passive observation window is `--for`

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5)
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl why --help
Why is this key silent — the non-verdict, itemised (#214).

A rung ladder over facts the engine already holds: scope reach, grammar, registry declaration, the liveliness roster, declared publishers, storage coverage, a stored value, freshness, admin reachability. Every rung answers established / not-established (with its reason) / NOT ASKED — "not asked" is never rendered as "no" (RFC 09 §5.1 O4), because silence is never a verdict (RFC 05 §3.1). "No publisher declared" never reads as a bug: publishers declare lazily, on the first publication (RFC 08 §6.1). The default run costs the control plane only; `--for` adds the one data-plane rung. Exit 0 = nothing found and everything checked looks healthy; 1 = a cause was established — the finding; 2 = the observation was impaired.

Usage: zenctl why [OPTIONS] [SELECTOR]

Arguments:
  [SELECTOR]
          Full wire selector to watch — this session is un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5). Defaults to all v1 data under the base: `<base>/v1/**`, which `**` being unable to cross an `@`-chunk makes media-safe and blind to the verbatim planes (RFC 03 §4 D2)

Options:
      --origin <ORIGIN>
          Only this origin (`h-…` or `@service`)

      --class <CLASS>
          Only this class: telemetry, state, or events

      --producer <PRODUCER>
          Only this producer

      --for <SECS>
          Listen passively for this many seconds — the one rung that costs the data plane (RFC 09 §5.1, the v1.18 frugality note). Without it the wire-heard rung reads "not asked", never "silent" (O4)

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl base list` discovers the bases in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.toml`), repeatable. Joined with the live bus as a union (RFC 08 §6.1): a producer's served slice wins, these files fill the gaps, and a disagreement is reported — never silently overwritten. With the bus unreachable they answer alone

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`)

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default, and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

